in @prism

in @mirror/grammar
in @text

# @epistemologic/property/glass_wall
#
# Substrate-pull made structural. The discipline that has lived in
# `feedback-no-new-rust.md` and AGENTS.md § The Glass Wall as a
# stylistic rule becomes a verifiable property of every grammar that
# compiles. The compiler enforces the boundary; convention stops
# being load-bearing.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-kintsugi.md:
#
#   `glass_wall(g)` asserts that any non-mirror grammar must be under
#   the `@io` namespace. The inversion is structural: the property
#   does not probe for Rust specifically; it checks whether g is
#   mirror-shaped (parses through the meta-glass). Anything that
#   ISN'T mirror — Rust, Python, Go, raw bytes, foreign binary
#   blobs, vendor SDKs — must be under @io. The discipline is
#   automatically extended to every non-mirror substrate, present
#   and future. No per-substrate special cases.
#
# The structural pair with halts:
#
#   halts(g)       — mirror grammars terminate by construction
#                    (sub-Turing escape from undecidability).
#   glass_wall(g)  — non-mirror grammars must be under @io
#                    (substrate-pull escape from undiscipline).
#
# Together: every escape is auditable. Cross-wall kintsugi (Task #80)
# will then self-shrink @io toward its irreducible minimum (blocking
# syscalls, hardware interrupts, opaque vendor primitives) by pulling
# non-mirror grammars across the wall once their halting behaviour
# becomes structurally provable.
#
# Decidability: both clauses are decidable at compile time.
# `is_mirror(g)` is one ref equality at the meta-glass altitude;
# `namespace(g)` is a substring check. No oracle needed; no parse re-
# attempt; the substrate already knows which meta-glass tokenised g.
#
# Sibling properties: halts(T) (the structural pair this property
# inverts — halts proves termination of mirror grammars; glass_wall
# proves containment of non-mirror grammars), io_safety(ast) (the
# per-call discipline at the @io boundary; glass_wall is the
# namespace-level discipline that gates which grammars can even host
# such calls).
#
# Spec: docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-kintsugi.md.
# Convention: AGENTS.md § The Glass Wall.

grammar @epistemologic/property/glass_wall {

  # Clause (a): the grammar is mirror-shaped. Delegates to the meta-
  # glass primitive @mirror/grammar.is_mirror(g): true iff g parses
  # through @mirror/grammar. Mirror grammars satisfy this trivially;
  # everything else fails. When this clause fires, the property
  # succeeds without consulting clause (b) — mirror grammars are not
  # required to live under @io.
  is_mirror_shaped(g: grammar) -> verdict { \ }

  # Clause (b): the grammar's namespace starts with "@io". The check
  # is `@text.starts_with(@mirror/grammar.namespace(g), "@io")`.
  # Required when clause (a) fails; trivially true for grammars
  # already under @io. This is the structural condition that catches
  # any non-mirror substrate trying to enter the boot tree outside
  # the @io kernel.
  under_io_namespace(g: grammar) -> verdict { \ }

  # The disjunction: a grammar is glass-walled iff it is mirror-shaped
  # OR it is under @io. Decidable by construction; both clauses are
  # one substrate query each. Reed's slip on Task #78's earlier
  # framing ("substantial Rust work in bootstrap") would have failed
  # this property at compile time — the spec itself could not have
  # put Rust above @io without being itself @io. The substrate
  # catches discipline failures at the type-system level, not in code
  # review.
  glass_wall(g: grammar) -> verdict { \ }
}

out is_mirror_shaped
out under_io_namespace
out glass_wall
out @epistemologic/property/glass_wall
